Author: Van Tran <940988+vantt@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Fri Jul 17 10:16:45 2026 +0700

    feat(runner): parallel batch drain-run over the frontier (fan-out-parallel-8)
    
    Turn runOnce's single-item sequential pass into a bounded, concurrent
    drain-run. Poll the full ready set, steer it through root-affinity, and
    dispatch a bounded wave (maxRoots x maxLeavesPerRoot) concurrently via the
    now-async worker/goal-check pipeline; each state mutation (claim, propose,
    park, outcome, friction) runs as its own atomic write-queue transaction so
    concurrent workers never interleave a raw event-log write. The claim
    resolves the root and decides ownership INSIDE the queued callback on a fresh
    view (never a pre-read snapshot), matching the proven race-free shape. After
    each wave settles, re-poll and refill up to the caps until nothing is
    in-flight and the frontier is empty; a per-item halt stops further refills.
    
    Ownership stays in-memory only for the drain-run (no event type, no schema
    field). Add an optional parallel.{maxRoots,maxLeavesPerRoot} config block
    (positive integers, in-code defaults when absent) validated additively in
    loadRunnerConfig. runOnce's return shape becomes {outcome:'drained',
    dispatched:[...], parked:[...]}; busy/idle/dry-run keep their shapes. Update
    bin/fgos-runner.mjs to print one line per dispatched item.
    
    New tests prove real overlap (interval intersection, not a delay proxy),
    bounded cap + refill + termination, and the two-tier leaf cap under a shared
    root owner. Full suite green (583).
    
    Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_016hFNgKkbDZ7rSv4wfR5pXB

